FUNCTION TEMPLATES (Interview Revision)
=========================================================

What is a Function Template?
----------------------------
A function template allows writing one generic function that works with
multiple data types.

Syntax

template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Usage

add(10,20);          // int
add(2.5,3.5);        // double

Compiler generates the required function automatically.

---------------------------------------------------------

Template Instantiation
----------------------
The compiler creates actual functions only when the template is used.

Example

template<typename T>
T square(T x)
{
    return x * x;
}

square(5);      -> int square(int)

square(2.5);    -> double square(double)

---------------------------------------------------------

Template Type Deduction
-----------------------
Compiler automatically deduces template arguments.

print(10);          // T = int
print(3.14);        // T = double

Explicit specification is also possible.

print<int>(10);

---------------------------------------------------------

Multiple Template Parameters
----------------------------

template<typename T, typename U>
auto add(T a, U b)
{
    return a + b;
}

add(10,5.5);

T = int
U = double

---------------------------------------------------------

typename vs class
-----------------

template<typename T>

template<class T>

Both are identical.

Modern C++ style prefers typename.

---------------------------------------------------------

Function Template Overloading
-----------------------------

template<typename T>
void print(T);

template<typename T>
void print(T,T);

Templates can be overloaded.

---------------------------------------------------------

Normal Function vs Template
---------------------------

void print(int);

template<typename T>
void print(T);

print(10);      // Calls normal function

print(3.5);     // Calls template

Rule:
A non-template function is preferred if both are equally good matches.

---------------------------------------------------------

Why are Templates Defined in Header Files?***********************
------------------------------------------

The compiler must see the complete template definition while performing
template instantiation.

Otherwise, linker errors occur.

---------------------------------------------------------

Advantages
----------

✓ Generic programming
✓ Code reuse
✓ Compile-time polymorphism
✓ Type safety
✓ No code duplication

---------------------------------------------------------

Interview Questions
-------------------

Q. What is template instantiation?
Compiler generates actual functions/classes from templates during compilation.

Q. Difference between function overloading and function templates?
Overloading uses multiple implementations.
Templates use one generic implementation.

Q. Difference between typename and class?
No difference in template parameter lists.

Q. Can function templates be overloaded?
Yes.

Q. Can normal and template functions coexist?
Yes. Normal function is preferred if both match equally.

Q. Are templates compile-time or runtime?
Compile-time.

